  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe Nelder-Mead simplex method is well-suited for non-smooth optimization and avoids gradient dependencies. This approach will explore the solution space more thoroughly than the previous first-order method, using a simplex of solutions that evolve through reflection, expansion, and contraction.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables:\n- h: step function represented as an n_points array\n- corr: cross-correlation between h and 1-h\nObjective: minimize max(corr) * dx, where corr is computed via np.correlate\nConstraints:\n- h[i] \u2208 [0, 1] for all i\n- sum(h) = n_points / 2 exactly\n- dx = 2.0 / n_points\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n1. Initialize a simplex of candidate solutions with random initialization\n2. Evaluate each candidate's C\u2085 value\n3. Perform Nelder-Mead iterations:\n   a. Order candidates by C\u2085 value\n   b. Compute centroid without worst candidate\n   c. Reflect, expand, or contract based on comparison of new point\n   d. Apply feasibility repair and constraints\n4. Track best candidate and ensure budget compliance\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Initial simplex size: 10 candidates\n- Random seed to ensure reproducibility\n- Start simplex with initial_h_values as a baseline candidate\n-_budget-aware termination conditions\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Estimated cost:Medium\n- Detailed progress checking\n- Budget-aware simplex size adjustment\n- Validity guarantees via feasibility repair\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFinal solution must satisfy:\n- sum(h) = n_points / 2 exactly\n- 0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n- C\u2085 computed exactly via supplied evaluator",
